# Dagric OS — declare the default browser, system-wide.
#
# THE BUG THIS FIXES, caught by booting the Pro ISO in QEMU and looking at the
# taskbar: free pins Firefox, Pro pins Chromium. Same panel configuration, same
# `x-www-browser` alternative (both point at firefox-esr) — the difference is
# that nothing anywhere declared a default, and Pro is the only edition with
# two browsers installed to choose between.
#
# Plasma resolves its `preferred://browser` launcher through the XDG mimeapps
# default for x-scheme-handler/http, NOT through Debian's update-alternatives.
# With no default set, it takes whichever handler it happens to find first, and
# on Pro that is Chromium.
#
# Which is the wrong answer, and wrong in the direction that matters. Firefox is
# the browser Dagric actually hardens: telemetry off, Pocket off, studies off,
# tracking protection on, uBlock Origin. Chromium as packaged has none of that.
# So the edition somebody PAID for was the one that quietly handed them an
# unhardened Google browser as the default for every link they click — while the
# manual, the guide and the sales page all say Firefox. Free was correct only by
# accident, because it has nothing else to pick.
#
# Chromium stays installed and one click away on Pro; it is offered, not
# defaulted. Declaring the default explicitly also means this cannot drift again
# when a future edition adds a third browser.
#
# /etc/xdg is the system-wide layer of the XDG spec, so any owner who changes
# their default browser in System Settings overrides this in their own
# ~/.config/mimeapps.list and is never overridden back.
[Default Applications]
x-scheme-handler/http=firefox-esr.desktop
x-scheme-handler/https=firefox-esr.desktop
x-scheme-handler/about=firefox-esr.desktop
x-scheme-handler/unknown=firefox-esr.desktop
text/html=firefox-esr.desktop
application/xhtml+xml=firefox-esr.desktop

# Double-clicking an ISO writes it to a USB stick.
#
# THE BUG, measured on a running install: a real Dagric ISO is detected as
# application/x-iso9660-image, and NOTHING on the image claimed that type —
#     xdg-mime query default application/x-iso9660-image  ->  (nothing)
# in a bare shell and in a full KDE session alike. Ark claims the neighbouring
# application/vnd.efi.iso and application/x-iso9660-appimage, which are
# different types; kde-mimeapps.list mentions only the appimage one. So an owner
# who downloaded an ISO double-clicked it and got no window, no error and no
# Open With prompt — on the one file type this product hands out.
#
# WHY THE USB WRITER AND NOT THE ARCHIVE MANAGER. Ark can list an ISO's contents
# and is a reasonable answer for a developer, but it is the wrong first answer
# here. The reason somebody on Dagric has an .iso on their disk is almost always
# that they are about to put it on a stick: a second machine, a friend's laptop,
# a lab of them. That is the job isoimagewriter does, it is why both editions
# ship it, and it is why the launcher already calls it "USB Writer" with rufus
# and balenaetcher in its keywords. Ark stays one right-click away in Open With,
# because it claims both types itself and dagric-app-names now gives the writer
# a MimeType= so both apps are listed.
#
# OPENING THE WRITER IS NOT DESTRUCTIVE, and this was checked by looking at it
# rather than by reasoning about it, because "the default double-click for .iso
# is now a drive-erasing tool" is the obvious objection and it deserved a real
# answer. Screenshot of the window a double-click produces, on this image, with
# a Dagric ISO loaded:
#   * "Write this ISO image:" — the path, already filled in, with its size.
#   * "To this USB drive:" — a dropdown that is DISABLED and reads "Please plug
#     in a USB drive".
#   * "Create" — GREYED OUT.
# There is no reachable destructive action at all until a stick is physically
# plugged in and picked. It is the same shape as Rufus, which is the tool this
# audience last used, and it is strictly safer than the previous behaviour only
# in the sense that the previous behaviour was nothing happening.
#
# BOTH TYPES, AND THIS IS NOT BELT-AND-BRACES — IT IS LOAD-BEARING. The two
# mime databases on this image disagree about the SAME FILE. Asked about one
# Dagric ISO:
#     xdg-mime query filetype   ->  application/x-iso9660-image
#     kmimetypefinder           ->  application/vnd.efi.iso
# xdg-utils and Qt's QMimeDatabase apply shared-mime-info's magic rules
# differently, and QMimeDatabase is the one that matters, because KIO is what
# actually opens the file when somebody double-clicks it in Dolphin. Pinning
# only the type xdg-mime reports would have left the real double-click path
# resolving to vnd.efi.iso and landing on Ark — a fix that tests green from a
# shell and does nothing on the desktop. Measured, not reasoned: pinning one
# type was tried first and the file did not open.
#
# It is also right on its own terms. A plain hybrid ISO and one carrying a
# GPT/EFI partition are genuinely different types, an owner cannot be expected
# to know which they downloaded, and two ISOs in one folder must not behave
# differently. Ark held the vnd.efi.iso default by mimeinfo.cache order; this
# takes it.
#
# /etc/xdg is XDG_CONFIG_DIRS, which outranks every mimeapps.list under
# /usr/local/share and /usr/share INCLUDING the desktop-prefixed
# kde-mimeapps.list — verified by measurement, not read off the spec. An owner
# who sets a different default in System Settings writes
# ~/.config/mimeapps.list, which outranks this, and is never overridden back.
application/x-iso9660-image=org.kde.isoimagewriter.desktop
application/vnd.efi.iso=org.kde.isoimagewriter.desktop

# A downloaded .deb opens the Package Installer, which is what we already claim.
#
# dagric-app-names renames gdebi to "Package Installer" and its comment says, in
# as many words, "Double-clicking a downloaded .deb opens this." That was not
# true. Upstream ships
#     /usr/share/applications/kde-mimeapps.list
#     application/vnd.debian.binary-package=org.kde.discover.desktop
# so double-clicking a .deb opened the Software Store instead — the app whose
# whole premise is a curated catalogue, handed a file from outside it. gdebi is
# installed on both editions, claims the type itself, and already carries
# Exec=gdebi-gtk %f; it was simply outranked.
#
# Measured, not assumed: gdebi-gtk was handed a real hello_2.10-5_amd64.deb in
# the live session and opened it. This pin is the only thing that was missing.
#
# Discover stays in Open With and remains the right tool for the catalogue. This
# only decides what a FILE does when you double-click it.
application/vnd.debian.binary-package=gdebi.desktop

# Text and source files open in the graphical editor this system ships.
#
# THE BUG: double-clicking a shell script opened vim. Not gvim — vim, from a
# .desktop carrying Terminal=true, so Plasma opens a Konsole window running a
# modal editor with no visible way out. For the audience this product is sold to
# that is not a poor default, it is a trap: someone who downloaded install.sh,
# double-clicked it expecting to read it, and cannot get their screen back.
#
# It was not one type. vim.desktop claims fifteen, and won every one of them
# except text/plain (which Kate already held): C, C++, their headers, Java,
# Pascal, Tcl, TeX, Makefiles, moc, text/english and shell scripts. So a .c file
# and a .txt file next to each other behaved completely differently.
#
# Kate is installed on both editions, is renamed "Text Editor" in the launcher,
# and is the editor every piece of Dagric documentation points at. It is a valid
# handler for all of these: application/x-shellscript and the text/x-* types are
# subclasses of text/plain in shared-mime-info, which Kate claims explicitly.
#
# vim is not removed and is not hidden — it stays installed, stays in the menu,
# and anyone who wants it back sets it once in System Settings, which writes
# ~/.config/mimeapps.list and outranks this file permanently. It is only no
# longer what a double-click reaches for on a graphical desktop.
application/x-shellscript=org.kde.kate.desktop
text/english=org.kde.kate.desktop
text/x-makefile=org.kde.kate.desktop
text/x-c=org.kde.kate.desktop
text/x-csrc=org.kde.kate.desktop
text/x-chdr=org.kde.kate.desktop
text/x-c++=org.kde.kate.desktop
text/x-c++src=org.kde.kate.desktop
text/x-c++hdr=org.kde.kate.desktop
text/x-java=org.kde.kate.desktop
text/x-moc=org.kde.kate.desktop
text/x-pascal=org.kde.kate.desktop
text/x-tcl=org.kde.kate.desktop
text/x-tex=org.kde.kate.desktop
